#!/bin/sh
# script/swish-check: boot the interactive system and type at the prompt.
#
#   script/swish-check                     all three architectures
#   script/swish-check --arch aarch64      one leg (aarch64, riscv64 or x86_64)
#   script/swish-check --release           the optimised build a stick carries (not in CI; x86_64 untried)
#   script/swish-check --input-race        the input driver holds 200 ms after each drain and Enter
#                                          follows each line by 50 ms (first boot only; aarch64 in CI)
#
# **`--input-race` is the input driver's proof.** Enter lands while the driver holds, the window
# in which a driver that cleared the PL011's interrupt after draining would strand a byte in the
# FIFO and never read input again. The cause and the runs are in notes/swish-check-flake.md.
#
# **It also gates the progenitor's stack** (milestone progenitor-stack (provisional)): the kernel
# prints `progenitor stack: N of M bytes at peak` each time the mark settles higher, this echoes
# each line with the command it followed, and a `BELOW` (less than two pages left) fails the run.
# notes/stack/progenitor-stack.md has the numbers and the frames.
#
# **The x86_64 leg (milestone 182) boots the UEFI image under OVMF**, the `BOOTX64.EFI` a customer
# copies to a USB stick, rather than a kernel under QEMU's PVH loader. It types the same
# script over COM1, the network lines included since 2026-10-05, when the kernel began building a
# stack on the `e1000e` the x86_64 runner attaches; `swish_check_omits` in
# `xtask/src/swish_check/verdict.rs` is where a leg-specific gap would say why. The four `uuid` lines run since milestone 595
# (the shell runs a std program), which gave the progenitor an entropy service on `RDSEED`. It is
# the slow leg: about six minutes on patagonia against about ninety seconds for the same script
# over PVH, because under firmware the console server tees every byte onto the screen and waits for
# it to be drawn: milestone 400 (the shell on the firmware's screen). Milestone 182 (x86_64's own
# interactive-boot entry point) has the measurement in its block, and why the slower boot was
# chosen.
#
# **The aarch64 and riscv64 legs launch the graphical terminal too** (2026-10-03 UTC, calef's
# ruling; until then `--graphical` and `--graphical-serial` were two more flags and
# `swish-check-graphical` was a CI job of its own, which cost a runner and about ten minutes of
# build for about a minute of boots; the flags are gone and refuse themselves if typed). Milestone
# 632 (graphics on demand, provisional), reversing milestone 177 (wire the graphical terminal
# stack into the real interactive boot)'s boot half, made the graphical boot the same `--features
# shell` UART system as every other boot, so the only differences are a virtio-gpu, typing
# `graphical_terminal` at the prompt, and reading the screen back (a QEMU screendump decoded
# against the same atlas the guest drew from). A launch ends the boot it is typed into (the
# shell waits on the session), so each leg is three boots, and a fourth for the USB keyboard:
#
#   1. The full script, **no gpu**: its `graphical_terminal` line requires the refusal sentence,
#      which is the headless half of milestone 632 (graphics on demand)'s claim, on all three ISAs, at no extra boot.
#   2. After the reboot: the short script, then a gpu and **no keyboard**, and `graphical_terminal`.
#      The session's keystrokes are the guest's own UART, read raw from the boot's line discipline
#      (`OPERATION_RAWMODE`/`OPERATION_READRAW`, the shell's own section 227 shape) and echoed by the session
#      (milestone 192 (a keyboard on real silicon), option A). That is the configuration all three
#      target boards have: argon, radon and xenon each have a serial line and none has a
#      virtio-input device. It is NOT a substitute for a bench run: QEMU has neither board's
#      framebuffer handoff (milestone 157 (U-Boot's simple-framebuffer handoff)) nor its real UART
#      timing.
#   3. A scriptless boot with a gpu **and a keyboard**, a key pressed with the monitor's `sendkey`
#      through the session's `keyboard_driver`. It leaves the NIC off: with every device attached a
#      launch peaks at 30 of 32 capability slots (`kernel/src/cap.rs`), a boot nothing else makes.
#   4. **A USB keyboard on an xHCI controller** (milestone 242 (USB host and HID), all three
#      architectures, x86_64 included): no script, `echo hello` pressed on the keyboard with
#      `sendkey`, and `hello` required back. Full speed, and on x86_64 MSI with no MSI-X, as
#      xenon's controller is. notes/usb.md.
#
# Both launches make the same two assertions, the session's `$ ` prompt on the screen and one key
# echoed beside it, and neither can tell which source the key came from, which is the property
# milestone 192's option A is finished by. They are the legs' full claim on the graphical path:
# nothing past one key is asserted there. See `launch_graphical_terminal`'s doc in
# `xtask/src/swish_check/launch.rs`. **x86_64 has no graphical boot** (its runner attaches no
# virtio-gpu; milestone 632's BUGS has what it would take) and stays on the refusal.
#
# This is the gate for `components/src/progenitor.rs`. Every other test that runs the shell has
# the KERNEL play the first process, so a broken progenitor fails nothing; the interactive boot is the only thing that
# runs the real one, and until this existed nothing ran the interactive boot. Milestone 50 hit three
# bugs that way and all three printed nothing at all. See notes/pipes.md.
#
# It is not part of script/test, because it builds the boot kernel beside the test one and boots it
# three times per ISA. It IS a `local` row in `script/ci-build`'s table (since milestone 230 (`script/shell-check` is red on `main`); that table was two
# lists until milestone 286), and the reason is what happened while nothing ran it: it went red on
# `main` on both architectures on 2026-08-28 and stayed red for five days, through every green
# check. A gate that exists, fails, and tells nobody is worse than no gate, and this one guards the
# only program in the tree that no other test executes.
#
# One row, two callers, which is what the `local` tier means. A person gets it from
# `script/ci-build` with no arguments and pays 30 seconds for the aarch64 and riscv64 legs against an
# already-warm target directory, plus about six minutes for x86_64's (above);
# CI gets it from `script/ci-build test swish-check`, appended to the `build + test` job rather than
# given a job of its own, so it reuses that job's build instead of paying ten minutes for thirty
# seconds of checking. The x86_64 leg changes that arithmetic: it adds roughly six minutes (more
# on a slower runner) to a job that took about 13 against its 30-minute timeout. Run it directly when you touch the progenitor, the shell, or what the boot
# grants them.
#
# BUGS
#
# **The transcript is written by two processes at once and can arrive shuffled.** The kernel prints
# its user-fault reports with its own UART driver; the userspace `console` server drives the same
# device from another address space; nothing arbitrates. So a thread dying while the progenitor prints
# splices the two lines together at byte granularity, nondeterministically: milestone 230 watched
# the same commit pass one CI run and fail the next, and watched `construction budget dropped` come
# out with a longest surviving run of five characters. That is a defect in the system rather than in
# this script, it has consequences well past this gate (anything reading a board's serial log), and
# it is proposed as its own milestone in `design/roadmap/0230-shell-check-is-red.md`.
#
# **Narrowed by milestone 342 (the kernel and the `console` server drive one UART from two address
# spaces), 2026-10-03.** Once the system log service attaches, the kernel's lines reach the UART
# through it and the console, which writes them only at a line start; `--flood` proves it (every
# flood line whole on both QEMU legs). What still splices: the kernel's lines between the console
# starting and the service attaching, a stalled service's counted fallback, and a panic, all
# three on purpose (`kernel/src/kernel_log.rs`'s BUGS).
#
# What this script does about it: the two boot-line checks put their teeth on the sentence the progenitor
# prints when the answer is NO, searched exactly, because interleaving can destroy a string and
# cannot create one. A line that cannot be read at all is reported rather than failed, but only when
# the kernel demonstrably wrote during the boot; with one writer there is nothing to shuffle, so a
# missing line is a real one and still fails. See `boot_claim` in
# `xtask/src/swish_check/transcript.rs`.
#
# **The cost of that choice, stated plainly: it can pass a boot it should have failed.** If the
# progenitor's
# report were deleted AND a thread faulted in the same boot, this reads the second as an explanation
# for the first. Both halves have to happen together and the second is itself a defect the
# transcript shows. The trade is deliberate: a false red taxes every lane whose change had nothing
# to do with it, and a false green here is undone by repetition, since a progenitor that stops dropping
# its budget prints the failing sentence on every boot of both legs on every push.
#
# **A killed user thread is not itself a failure here**, which is how `login` dying at `_start` on
# every boot went unnoticed through passing runs (`components/src/login.rs`'s own BUGS). That assertion is
# the obvious next ratchet and belongs with the fix for it.
#
# **The two graphical launches prove one key, not the script.** They are green since milestone 177
# found the hang that kept them red (the boot never received two drivers' one-time reports, so both
# drivers sat in a blocking SEND; notes/framebuffer-contract.md has the evidence). What they check is
# the launched session's prompt on the screen and one typed key echoed beside it; nothing past that
# is asserted on the graphical path. Folding them in made the plain legs' gauges read them, which is
# how the capability-slot record turned out to be two short (`kernel/src/cap.rs`).
#
# Name: ratified 2026-09-23 (calef, replacing `shell-check`, which was provisional and introduced
# 2026-08-02). It boots the kernel and types at the prompt, so it
# is named for the program actually driven, the way `coremark` and `compositor` are. The old name had
# two problems and the first is the one that bit. **It was one hyphen from `shellcheck`**, the shell
# linter `script/lint` itself runs, so two names differed by punctuation while naming unrelated
# things, and a reader who had met the linter met this and was wrong about it. That cost a real
# boot: on 2026-09-23 a lane briefed explicitly not to start QEMU ran `script/shell-check` believing
# it was a shell-script linter and booted a 109-minute bounded emulator. Second, the word it was
# built from is stale: design/naming.md's BUGS records that `shell` no longer names any program in
# this tree, because the shell is `swish`. Refused `prompt-check` (it names a surface rather than the
# binary, and this family is named for what it drives).
#
# **The cargo feature is still spelled `shell`, deliberately.** That is a different name in a
# different namespace, it is not the one that misleads a reader at the command line, and renaming it
# touches every `--features` invocation in the tree. It keeps the stale vocabulary until somebody
# prices that sweep on its own.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh
exec cargo xtask swish-check "$@"
